SUMMARY:Domestic Violence\, Storytelling\, and Social Change Workshop wit
 h Filmmaker Natalie Pattillo
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, October 232:30 - 3:30 PM  In-Person\, Lindley Hall 1
 02  In recognition of Domestic Violence Awareness Month\, join award-winn
 ing documentary filmmaker Natalie Pattillo for a powerful discussion on d
 omestic violence.  The conversation will explore the criminalization of s
 urvivors\, the role of institutions and public policy\, and how communica
 tion and storytelling can raise awareness\, shape public opinion\, and dr
 ive social change.Participants will explore and engage in powerful discus
 sions about:Why Storytelling Matters: How stories can make complex system
 s easier to understand\, build empathy\, shift public perception\, and he
 lp people imagine better solutions.  Building Community Around Survivors:
  How friends\, families\, advocates\, workplaces\, legal teams\, service 
 providers\, and institutions can support survivors and help them feel les
 s isolated.  Seeing the Whole System: How housing\, employment\, food acc
 ess\, child care\, education\, health care\, law\, and public policy can 
 impact survivors\, and how meaningful change will require us looking acro
 ss systems together.  Collaboration and Ethics: Drawing from documentary 
 practice and trauma-informed guidance to explore how people across fields
  can create environments that prioritize survivors' safety\, agency\, tru
 st\, and choice while navigating consent\, boundaries\, power\, and confi
 dentiality.    —** You can also join Natalie Pattillo on Friday\, Octob
 er 23 at 1:30 PM for a Master Class in Documentary Filmmaking (https://ev
 ents.iu.edu/kinsey/event/2383524-master-class-in-documentary-filmmaking-w
 ith).  Natalie's documentary film\, And So I Stayed will screen at the IU
  Cinema on Saturday\, October 24 at 7:00 PM. **Natalie Pattillo is an awa
 rd-winning documentary filmmaker\, writer and journalist whose work explo
 res education\, child care access\, gender-based violence\, food and cult
 ure. Her work has appeared in The New York Times\, MSNBC\, VICE\, New Yor
 k Magazine\, Al Jazeera America\, Salon and beyond. In 2020\, she receive
 d the Media Award from the National Coalition Against Domestic Violence. 
 She was also named a Faculty News Champion by the Center for Community Ne
 ws at the University of Vermont for her work supporting student journalis
 m and rebuilding local news.  Natalie was the co-director\, writer and pr
 oducer of And So I Stayed (https://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.com
 /?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2F2021%2F06%2F11%2Fmovies%2Fdomestic-
 violence-law.html&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c4021
 35b208def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C63921709237
 9681366%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwM
 CIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sda
 ta=%2Fi1Tl7bk%2F8Acy%2B5RDgCzQCc%2F7gSL9gR5STvkaDCSvoQ%3D&amp\;reserved=0
 )\, an award-winning documentary about survivors of domestic violence who
  were incarcerated for acts of survival. The film has been featured by Th
 e New York Times\, the Chicago Tribune and ABC News.  She is currently wo
 rking on Writing for My Mother (https://nam12.safelinks.protection.outloo
 k.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.documentaries.org%2Ffilms%2Fwriting-for-my-m
 other%2F&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b208def
 48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379713731%7
 CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOi
 JXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=HJDUrV
 kh4JRuRMGml0LyUARQNxyWimVxZrLDyPnKf38%3D&amp\;reserved=0)\, a documentary
  and book project exploring literacy in the United States\, the communiti
 es that have been excluded from educational opportunities and what become
 s possible in people's lives and for our democracy when those barriers ar
 e broken down.  Natalie lives in Providence\, Rhode Island\, with her hus
 band and three children. In her free time\, she can usually be found gard
 ening\, knitting\, reading\, or taking unhurried walks.AND SO I STAYEDis 
 an eye-opening and award-winning documentary about three survivors of abu
 se fighting for their lives and how the legal system betrayed them.These 
 women paid a steep price with long prison sentences\, lost time with love
 d ones\, and painful memories. Formerly incarcerated survivor-advocate Ki
 m Dadou Brown\, who met her wife while incarcerated\, is a driving force 
 in the passage of New York's Domestic Violence Survivors Justice Act (DVS
 JA)\, a new law meant to prevent survivors from receiving harsh prison se
 ntences for their acts of survival. Nikki Addimando\, a mother of two you
 ng children\, suffered the consequences when a judge didn't follow the la
 w's guidelines. Tanisha Davis\, a single mother who was ripped away from 
 her son in 2013\, is hopeful the new law is her way out of a harsh prison
  sentence.AND SO I STAYED aims to hold the legal system and public accoun
 table by exposing the unjust misogyny and racism embedded in these power 
 structures while also portraying the resilience\, hopes\, and dreams of s
 urvivors and their loved ones.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Fri
 day\, October 23</strong></h5><h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>2:
 30 - 3:30 PM</strong></h5><p style="text-align: center\;">  In-Person\, L
 indley Hall 102</p><p>  In recognition of Domestic Violence Awareness Mon
 th\, join award-winning documentary filmmaker Natalie Pattillo for a powe
 rful discussion on domestic violence.</p><p class="font-claude-response-b
 ody break-words whitespace-normal" dir="ltr">  The conversation will expl
 ore the criminalization of survivors\, the role of institutions and publi
 c policy\, and how communication and storytelling can raise awareness\, s
 hape public opinion\, and drive social change.</p><p><em><strong>Particip
 ants will explore and engage in powerful discussions about:</strong></em>
 </p><ul><li><strong>Why Storytelling Matters:</strong> How stories can ma
 ke complex systems easier to understand\, build empathy\, shift public pe
 rception\, and help people imagine better solutions.  </li><li><strong>Bu
 ilding Community Around Survivors:</strong> How friends\, families\, advo
 cates\, workplaces\, legal teams\, service providers\, and institutions c
 an support survivors and help them feel less isolated.  </li><li><strong>
 Seeing the Whole System:</strong> How housing\, employment\, food access\
 , child care\, education\, health care\, law\, and public policy can impa
 ct survivors\, and how meaningful change will require us looking across s
 ystems together.  </li><li><strong>Collaboration and Ethics:</strong> Dra
 wing from documentary practice and trauma-informed guidance to explore ho
 w people across fields can create environments that prioritize survivors'
  safety\, agency\, trust\, and choice while navigating consent\, boundari
 es\, power\, and confidentiality.  </li></ul><p class="font-claude-respon
 se-body break-words whitespace-normal" dir="ltr" style="text-align: cente
 r\;">  —</p><p style="text-align: center\;"><em>**</em> You can also jo
 in Natalie Pattillo on Friday\, October 23 at 1:30 PM for a <a href="http
 s://events.iu.edu/kinsey/event/2383524-master-class-in-documentary-filmma
 king-with" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Master Class in Documentary Fil
 mmaking</a>.</p><p style="text-align: center\;">  Natalie's documentary f
 ilm\, <a href="https://events.iu.edu/kinsey/event/2383529-and-so-i-stayed
 -with-filmmaker-natalie-pattillo-at-t" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em
 >And So I Stayed</em></a> will screen at the IU Cinema on Saturday\, Octo
 ber 24 at 7:00 PM. **</p><p><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><str
 ong>Natalie Pattillo</strong> is an award-winning documentary filmmaker\,
  writer and journalist whose work explores education\, child care access\
 , gender-based violence\, food and culture. Her work has appeared in The 
 New York Times\, MSNBC\, VICE\, New York Magazine\, Al Jazeera America\, 
 Salon and beyond. In 2020\, she received the Media Award from the Nationa
 l Coalition Against Domestic Violence. She was also named a Faculty News 
 Champion by the Center for Community News at the University of Vermont fo
 r her work supporting student journalism and rebuilding local news.</span
 ></p><p>  Natalie was the co-director\, writer and producer of <em><a cla
 ss="external" title="Original URL: https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/11/mov
 ies/domestic-violence-law.html. Click or tap if you trust this link." hre
 f="https://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.
 nytimes.com%2F2021%2F06%2F11%2Fmovies%2Fdomestic-violence-law.html&amp\;d
 ata=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b208def48f0787%7C1113b
 e34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379681366%7CUnknown%7CTWFp
 bGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOI
 joiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=%2Fi1Tl7bk%2F8Acy%2B5
 RDgCzQCc%2F7gSL9gR5STvkaDCSvoQ%3D&amp\;reserved=0" target="_blank" rel="n
 oopener noreferrer" data-auth="NotApplicable" data-linkindex="0">And So I
  Stayed</a></em>\, an a<span class="x_gmail_default">ward-winning</span> 
 documentary about survivors of domestic violence who were incarcerated fo
 r acts of survival. The film has been featured by The New York Times\, th
 e Chicago Tribune and ABC News.</p><p>  She is currently working on <em><
 a class="external" title="Original URL: https://www.documentaries.org/fil
 ms/writing-for-my-mother/. Click or tap if you trust this link." href="ht
 tps://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.docum
 entaries.org%2Ffilms%2Fwriting-for-my-mother%2F&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolf
 es%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b208def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd0
 2510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379713731%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1
 hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoy
 fQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=HJDUrVkh4JRuRMGml0LyUARQNxyWimVxZrLDyPnK
 f38%3D&amp\;reserved=0" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-au
 th="NotApplicable" data-linkindex="1">Writing for My Mother</a></em>\, a 
 documentary and book project exploring literacy in the United States\, th
 e communities that have been excluded from educational opportunities and 
 what becomes possible in people's lives and for our democracy when those 
 barriers are broken down.</p><p>  Natalie lives in Providence\, Rhode Isl
 and\, with her husband and three children. In her free time\, she can usu
 ally be found gardening\, knitting\, reading<span class="x_gmail_default"
 >\,</span> or <span class="x_gmail_default">taking unhurried walks.</span
 ></p><p><span class="s1"><a class="external" href="https://andsoistayedfi
 lm.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><picture class="lw_image 
 lw_image32013 lw_align_left"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://ev
 ents.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/199/width/150/height/103/32013_And_So
 _I_Stayed_Hi-Res_Prouduction_Still_01.rev.1786929094.webp 2x\, https://ev
 ents.iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/199/width/150/height/103/32013_And_So
 _I_Stayed_Hi-Res_Prouduction_Still_01.rev.1786929094.webp 3x" data-origin
 ="responsive"/><source type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/li
 ve/image/scale/2x/gid/199/width/150/height/103/32013_And_So_I_Stayed_Hi-R
 es_Prouduction_Still_01.rev.1786929094.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.edu/liv
 e/image/scale/3x/gid/199/width/150/height/103/32013_And_So_I_Stayed_Hi-Re
 s_Prouduction_Still_01.rev.1786929094.jpg 3x" data-origin="responsive"/><
 img width="150" height="103" alt="And So I Stayed" data-alt="And So I Sta
 yed" src="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/199/width/150/height/103/3
 2013_And_So_I_Stayed_Hi-Res_Prouduction_Still_01.rev.1786929094.jpg" titl
 e="And So I Stayed Hi-Res Prouduction Still 01" srcset="https://events.iu
 .edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/199/width/150/height/103/32013_And_So_I_Stay
 ed_Hi-Res_Prouduction_Still_01.rev.1786929094.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.
 edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/199/width/150/height/103/32013_And_So_I_Staye
 d_Hi-Res_Prouduction_Still_01.rev.1786929094.jpg 3x" data-max-w="3200" da
 ta-max-h="2200" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></picture>AND SO I 
 STAYED</strong></a></span><span class="s1">is an eye-opening and award-wi
 nning documentary about three survivors of abuse fighting for their lives
  and how the legal system betrayed them.</span></p><p class="p1"><span cl
 ass="s1">These women paid a steep price with long prison sentences\, lost
  time with loved ones\, and painful memories. Formerly incarcerated survi
 vor-advocate Kim Dadou Brown\, who met her wife while incarcerated\, is a
  driving force in the passage of New York's Domestic Violence Survivors J
 ustice Act (DVSJA)\, a new law meant to prevent survivors from receiving 
 harsh prison sentences for their acts of survival. Nikki Addimando\, a mo
 ther of two young children\, suffered the consequences when a judge didn'
 t follow the law's guidelines. Tanisha Davis\, a single mother who was ri
 pped away from her son in 2013\, is hopeful the new law is her way out of
  a harsh prison sentence.</span></p><p class="p1"><span class="s1"><span 
 data-olk-copy-source="MailCompose">AND SO I STAYED aims to hold the legal
  system and public accountable by exposing the unjust misogyny and racism
  embedded in these power structures while also portraying the resilience\
 , hopes\, and dreams of survivors and their loved ones.</span></span></p>
